For Python, the same multi-stage concept applies, but Python doesn't have pom.xml or target/.

Suppose your project is:
myapp/
├── requirements.txt
├── app.py
└── ...

A simple multi-stage Dockerfile can be:
# Stage 1: Build
FROM python:3.12-slim AS build
WORKDIR /app
COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip install --prefix=/install -r requirements.txt
COPY . .

# Stage 2: Runtime
FROM python:3.12-slim
WORKDIR /app
COPY --from=build /install /usr/local                 Copy dependencies from Stage 1
COPY --from=build /app .                             Copy application code from Stage 1
CMD ["python", "app.py"]



Understand the flow
Stage 1: Build
│
├── requirements.txt
├── pip install
├── Python dependencies
└── application code
        │
        ▼
Stage 2: Runtime
│
├── Python
├── installed dependencies
└── app.py
        │
        ▼
CMD ["python", "app.py"]





Compare with Java

Java:
pom.xml
   ↓
mvn package
   ↓
target/app.jar
   ↓
Java runtime
   ↓
java -jar app.jar

Python:
requirements.txt
   ↓
pip install
   ↓
Python dependencies
   ↓
Python runtime
   ↓
python app.py

There is no target/ folder in Python because Python normally runs the .py application directly.
Also, unlike your React example, Nginx is not required here. A Python backend such as Flask/FastAPI runs with a Python application server/runtime.




--prefix=/install tells pip:
Install the Python packages into the /install directory instead of the normal Python package location.
This is useful in a multi-stage Docker build because we can copy those installed packages from Stage 1 to Stage 2.

Without --prefix
RUN pip install -r requirements.txt
Packages are installed into Python's normal location inside Stage 1.

With --prefix
RUN pip install --prefix=/install -r requirements.txt

Packages go into something like:

/app
/install
   └── lib/
       └── python3.12/
           └── site-packages/
               ├── flask
               ├── requests
               └── ...

Then Stage 2:

COPY --from=build /install /usr/local
copies those installed packages into the runtime image.
So --prefix=/install basically gives us a separate folder containing the dependencies, making it easy to transfer them to the final image.

Install Python dependencies
RUN pip install --prefix=/install -r requirements.txt
This is the important line.
-r requirements.txt means:
Read requirements.txt and install all the packages listed there.
For example, if:
requirements.txt

contains:
flask
requests

then pip installs Flask and Requests.
What does --prefix=/install do?
Instead of installing the packages into the normal Python location, we tell pip:
Put the installed packages under /install.
Conceptually:
Build stage
/install
   ↓
Python packages
   ├── Flask
   ├── Requests
   └── dependencies
Why?
Because later we can copy /install from the build stage into the runtime stage.



requirements.txt is a Python file that lists the packages/dependencies your application needs.
Example:
Flask==3.1.0
requests==2.32.3
pandas==2.2.3

It is similar to Java's:
pom.xml
Comparison

Java:
pom.xml
   ↓
Maven reads it
   ↓
Downloads dependencies
   ↓
Builds app.jar

Python:
requirements.txt
   ↓
pip reads it
   ↓
Downloads dependencies
   ↓
Dependencies installed

So in our Dockerfile:
COPY requirements.txt .
RUN pip install --prefix=/install -r requirements.txt

means:
Copy requirements.txt into the build container.
pip reads the file.
Install all listed Python packages into /install.

Then Stage 2 copies those installed packages:

COPY --from=build /install /usr/local
Important: requirements.txt itself is only a list. It is not the dependency. The actual packages are installed from that list.





